pychfpga: ICEBoard & FPGA-based correlator control
==================================================



Prerequisites
-------------

Before you start, you need:
	- a ssh key loaded in ssh_agent that allows you to access your bitbucket account::

		    eval `ssh-agent -s`
		    ssh-add path_to_bitbucket_key

	- Permissions to the pychfpga and dependent repositories in bitbucket. Ask McGill administrator.
	- git LFS (git >1.8.2 includes LFS by default)
	- Python 2.7 (Python 3 **not** supported)
	- pip 18.1 (2018-10-05) or later (to support the PEP 508 dependency specs)
	- mdns/avahi/bonjour development libraries should be installed to allow the python package pybonjour to install::

		sudo apt install  libavahi-compat-libdnssd1  # on Ubuntu

Obtaining and installing ``pychfpga``
------------------------------------

Get the ``pychfpga`` repository::

	$ git lfs clone git@bitbucket.org:winterlandcosmology/pychfpga.git  # add the ``-b`` option to specify a branch

Create a clean python environment and install the `pychfpga` package all its dependencies::

	$ virtualenv my_env  # create new virtual environment named "my_env". You can clear an existing environment with "virtualenv --clear my_env"
	$ source my_env/bin/activate  # activate the new environment
	$ pip install -e ./pychfpga  # install in-place


Manual operation through `fpga_array`
-------------------------------------

Make sure you have ipython installed in your current environment::

	$ source my_env/bin/activate  # activate the python environment
	$ pip install ipython

Manually starting up the system with fpga_array
-----------------------------------------------

`fpga_array` creates an object that represents any number of ICEBoards and ICECrates and allow their initialization. Once started, it returns control to you. (See `fpga_master` for a full interactive server). First start ipython::

	source my_env/bin/activate  # activate the python environment
	cd pychfpga
	ipython

Assuming you have a single board with IP address 1.2.3.4 and want to use an external correlator that will process the data from the two QSFP+ links (mode `shuffle16`):


	run -i fpga_array 1.2.3.4 --prog --open --sync_method local_soft_trigger --mode shuffle16

If you have avahi, you can specify the board model and serial number. In the following example, we start the board with the stand-alone firmware correlator (more `corr16`) that will stream integrated data over the 1G Ethernet SFP+ link::

	run -i fpga_array mb 123 --prog --open --sync_method local_soft_trigger --mode corr16

With avahi present, you can select an ensemble of boards by crate model and serial number (here boards on 16-slot backplane model MGK7BP16 serial 12 and 13). We itialize 2 crates (32 boards, 512 analog inputs) and corner-turn engine for use with an external correlator farm (mode shuffle512), and synchronized together through IRIG-B:

	run -i fpga_array bp16 12 13 --prog --open --sync_method distributed_time --mode corr512


`fpga_master` and `raw_acq`
---------------------------

Prerequisites
+++++++++++++

	- If you expect to receive raw ADC, FFT or correlator packets from the IceBoard (using `raw_acq.py`, for example), you need to enable jumbo frames and increase buffer size. The following commands do that for the current session. You might want to make those changes permanent::

		sudo ifconfig eth0 mtu 9000 # replace "eth0" with your interface name
        sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=26214400
        sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_default=26214400


	- Install Prometheus and Grafana if you want to view the system status graphs published by `fpga_master`

	- You need a YAML configuration file

Operation
+++++++++

First, make sure you are in the pychfpga folder and in the right python environment::

	source my_env/bin/activate  # activate the python environment
	cd pychfpga

`raw_acq` starts a web server can receive raw data  from the ICeBoards (ADC, FFT, firmware correlator) and store those to disk, and also publishes metrics for Prometheus. To start the raw_acq server::

	./raw_acq path_to_config_file:jfc.drao

where `jfc.drao` is the section in the configuration file that contains a raw_acq definition.

To start the fpga_master server::

	./fpga_master path_to_config_file:jfc.drao

